L000555	\emph{Appendix A: Proof of linearity of steady-state probabilities.}---We use Eq.~\eqref{eq:response_prob} to calculate the response of steady-state probabilities subjected to perturbations in the rates $\kpm$. We consider the matrix
L000556	\begin{align}
L000557	 \mat{W}_{i} = \begin{pNiceMatrix}[last-col,first-row]
L000558	  & i  & j  & & \\
L000559	  \ddots& &  & &\\
L000560	  & 1 & 1 & & i\\[1ex]
L000561	  & \kp & -\km-\ldots & & j  \\
L000562	       &  &  & \ddots & \\
L000563	\end{pNiceMatrix}
L000564	\label{app_eq:W_n}
L000565	\end{align}
L000566	in which the appearance of input rates $\kpm$ is reduced to only two entries: $(i,j)$ and the diagonal element $(j,j)$. We find
L000567	\begin{align}
L000568	  \partial_{\kp} p_n &= - \left[\mat{W}^{-1}_{i}\right]_{n,j} p_{i} = - \frac{\left[\adj\left(\mat{W}_{i}\right)\right]_{n,j}}{\det\left( \mat{W}_{i}\right)}  p_{i} \nonumber \\
L000569	  \partial_{\km} p_n &=  \left[\mat{W}^{-1}_{i}\right]_{n,j} p_{j} =  \frac{\left[\adj\left(\mat{W}_{i}\right)\right]_{n,j}}{\det\left( \mat{W}_{i}\right)}  p_{j}
L000570	  \label{eq:response}
L000571	\end{align}
L000572	for an arbitrary vertex $n$. The second equalities are obtained by exploiting the relation between adjugate matrix $\adj\left(\mat{W}_{i}\right)$, with $\left[\adj(\W)\right]_{n, m} \equiv (-1)^{n+m}\det\left(\mat{W}_{i\backslash (m,n)}\right)$---this is equivalent to the transpose of the cofactor matrix of $\W$---and inverse of $\mat{W}_i$
L000573	\begin{align}
L000574	  \adj\left(\mat{W}_{i}\right) =  \mat{W}^{-1}_{i}\det\left( \mat{W}_{i}\right).
L000575	  \label{eq:adj}
L000576	\end{align}
L000577	As our main results, we find from Eqs.~\eqref{eq:response} and~\eqref{eq:adj}
L000578	\begin{align}
L000579	  \frac{\partial_{\kpm} p_n}{\partial_{\kpm} p_m} = \frac{\left[\adj\left(\W\right)\right]_{n, j}}{\left[\adj\left(\W\right)\right]_{m, j}} \equiv \chi_{n,m}.
L000580	\end{align}
L000581	The mutual linearity relation in Eq.~\eqref{eq:linearity} then follows immediately. The relation holds except if $p_m = 0$ for all $\kpm$, which is generally impossible for irreducible graphs unless transition rates are infinitely large.
L000582	
L000583	\emph{Appendix B: Probability bounds for unidirectional control.}---While the bounds in Eq.~\eqref{eq:bounds} also hold if only one of the input rates is controlled or if the edge is totally irreversible, the bound corresponding to the fixed (or zero) rate overestimates the range of accessible values. Instead, $\lim_{\kpm \to 0} p_n$ results in the bounds
L000584	\begin{align}
L000585	  \mathcal{U}^+_n \equiv  \frac{\tau_{n\backslash i \to j} }{\sum_l \tau_{l \backslash i\to j}} \qquad \mathcal{U}^-_n \equiv \frac{\tau_{n\backslash j\to i} }{\sum_l \tau_{l \backslash j \to i}},
L000586	  \label{eq:tight_bounds}
L000587	\end{align}
L000588	that  limit all possible solutions. Note that writing $\tau_{n\backslash i\to j} = \tau_{n\backslash i\leftrightarrow j} + \km \tau_{n}^{/ j\to i}$, and analog for $\tau_{n\backslash j\to i}$, the bounds are independent of the controlled rate. Analytical expressions for the affine coefficients and susceptibilities follow from Eq.~\eqref{eq:offset_suscs} upon replacing $\mathcal{B}_{\lbrace n,m\rbrace}^\pm \to \mathcal{U}_{\lbrace n,m\rbrace}^\mp$ for the respective cases.
